  "title": "Assault on the Death Star",
  "section": "Movie Facts # 9 of 10",
  "description": "Because 20th Century Fox had little interest in licensing Star Wars for anything more ambitious than T-shirts and a few posters, Lucas marketing director Charles Lippincott was forced to look elsewhere. He secured deals with Stan Lee, Roy Thomas and Marvel for a comic book adaptation and with Del Rey Books for a novelization. An arrangement with toy company Kenner soon followed. Concerned that Star Wars would be beaten out by other summer films, such as Universal's Smokey and the Bandit, the studio demanded that theaters order the sci-fi flick if they wanted an eagerly-anticipated Fox production based on a best-selling novel from the era, The Other Side of Midnight."
